# Rank 1128 / CVE-2026-26314: go-ethereum's secp256k1 IsOnCurve check
# accepted (x, y) coordinates without first confirming they were reduced
# modulo the field prime, so an attacker-supplied point with a coordinate
# ≥ P could satisfy the curve equation only because Go's modular arithmetic
# silently wraps it, letting an out-of-canonical-range point pass as a valid
# curve point (CWE-20).
#
# IsOnCurve in crypto/secp256k1/curve.go computed y2 = y*y mod P and the
# right-hand side of y2 = x3 + b mod P directly from the caller's x and y,
# with no check that x < P and y < P first. Because every intermediate value
# is reduced mod P anyway, a coordinate an attacker chose to be P plus some
# offset produces the same reduced result as the canonical coordinate, so
# the equation can still balance for a point that is not the one it claims
# to be, an "invalid curve point" confusion that can corrupt downstream
# scalar multiplication and signature/key-derivation logic relying on
# IsOnCurve as a genuine membership test.
#
# Pool commit 895a8597cb16 is the real fix (COMMIT_OK, its own title names
# the mechanism). It adds `if x.Cmp(P) >= 0 || y.Cmp(P) >= 0 { return false }`
# at the top of IsOnCurve, the nocgo build's own IsOnCurve override in
# signature_nocgo.go, and the equivalent guard in the cgo path's C helper,
# secp256k1_fe_set_b32_limit's return value now checked instead of ignored.
# No pool correction.
#
# ATTENTION, vocabulary gap. Scanning either revision reports 0 findings.
# code.EcdhPeerPublicKeyMissingCurveValidation is the closest concept in the
# ontology, sharing the same theme, using a point in curve arithmetic without
# confirming it is a genuine member of the curve, but its condition assumes
# IsOnCurve itself is trustworthy and asks only whether it was called before
# a scalar multiplication; this bug is one layer beneath that, IsOnCurve's
# own precondition on its input coordinates is what is missing, and neither
# it nor code.EcdsaSignatureScalarBoundsMissing (the other CWE_347 sibling,
# for signature r/s range checks rather than point coordinates) has a Go
# binding. Stating the general shape, a modular-arithmetic membership test
# accepts an input outside the field's canonical representative range because
# every step re-reduces it anyway, needs recognizing that a value entering
# a mod-P computation was never itself checked to be less than P, a numeric
# range relationship distinct from the checks either existing concept states.
#
# No definition change attempted.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap, both revisions rejected.

test "rank1128: IsOnCurve accepts coordinates without checking they're reduced mod P"
  lang go
  file curve.go
  reject_review code.EcdhPeerPublicKeyMissingCurveValidation
  code
  ```
  package secp256k1

  import "math/big"

  func (bitCurve *BitCurve) IsOnCurve(x, y *big.Int) bool {
      y2 := new(big.Int).Mul(y, y)
      y2.Mod(y2, bitCurve.P)

      y23 := new(big.Int).Mul(x, x)
      y23.Mul(y23, x)

      y23.Add(y23, bitCurve.B)
      y23.Mod(y23, bitCurve.P)

      return y2.Cmp(y23) == 0
  }
  ```

test "rank1128: IsOnCurve rejects coordinates at or past the field prime first"
  lang go
  file curve.go
  reject_review code.EcdhPeerPublicKeyMissingCurveValidation
  code
  ```
  package secp256k1

  import "math/big"

  func (bitCurve *BitCurve) IsOnCurve(x, y *big.Int) bool {
      if x.Cmp(bitCurve.P) >= 0 || y.Cmp(bitCurve.P) >= 0 {
          return false
      }

      y2 := new(big.Int).Mul(y, y)
      y2.Mod(y2, bitCurve.P)

      y23 := new(big.Int).Mul(x, x)
      y23.Mul(y23, x)

      y23.Add(y23, bitCurve.B)
      y23.Mod(y23, bitCurve.P)

      return y2.Cmp(y23) == 0
  }
  ```
